  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1243 PM MDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nMain concern this weekend will be the heat, as an upper level ridge \nof the Desert southwest continues to bring hot temperatures today \nand Sunday. Current surface observations already show temperatures \nranging from upper-80s to upper-90s across the plains. With us \nreaching 90s before noon today, it looks like we will be on track \nfor temperatures to reach the upper 90s to low 100s by this \nafternoon. The only caveats would be incoming smoke or outflows from \nvirga showers keeping us just shy of forecasted highs. However, even \nif we are a few degrees shy, we will still be reaching Heat Advisory \ncriteria, and thus will keep it going today. Overnight lows will \nbe exceptionally warm, with guidance indicating some areas not \nreaching below 80 dg until 10/11 PM. \n\nCurrent satellite imagery shows cumulus developing over the high \nterrain and Palmer Divide area, with a few virga showers already \npopping up. Expect this to continue into the late afternoon/early \nevening across the high terrain and the plains. Given the dry air \nmass in place in the lower levels, most precipitation should not \nreach the ground. However, a few of the stronger storms could wet \nthe ground. ACARS profiles show an inverted-V sounding, with DCAPE \nvalues reaching up to 1500-1800 J/kg, so gusty outflows are expected \nwith these virga showers. Most gusts should be up to 45-50 mph, but \ncan't rule out an isolated severe gust. \n\nElevated fire weather conditions will continue this afternoon for \nthe high terrain, where relative humidity values have already \ndropped to below 20% and winds gusting up to 20 mph so far. Guidance \nstill indicates the potential for westerly winds gusting up to 25-30 \nmph, particularly for the northern mountains and North Park. \nHowever, these stronger winds should be short in duration. \n\nSunday will see temperatures a degree or two cooler from Saturday, \nbut it will still feel very hot with highs around 98-99 in Denver \nand low 100s for most of the eastern plains. The Heat Advisory will \ncontinue into the evening. It looks like we are on track to tie or \nbreak the maximum temperature record for August 9 (98 dg set in \n2021), however another round of smoke and/or virga showers could \nkeep us shy of those temperatures. Modeled Skew-T soundings still \nindicate an inverted-V sounding, so don't expect much, if any, \nprecipitation to reach the ground. However, with the increase in mid-\nlevel moisture, do expect these virga showers to be a bit more \nwidespread than Saturday. With any of these storms, strong outflow \nwinds will be the main concern. \n\nTemperatures in the mid/upper 90s still likely Monday and through \nmidweek, with daily chances of scattered afternoon virga \nshowers/thunderstorms. Best precipitation chances will be for the \nhigh terrain, but still can't rule out a few showers spilling onto \nthe plains. This slow increase in moisture through the week could \nproduce localized heavy rainfall in the mountains with the strongest \nstorms. WPC does have a Marginal Risk for excessive rainfall each \nday for this reason.\n\nit is not until the later half of the week that we could see a \nsignificant difference in temperatures and widespread precipitation \nchances. Ensemble guidance is honing in on the breakdown of the \nupper level ridge by Wednesday/Thursday, where southwest flow aloft \nwill bring in better monsoonal moisture. Precipitable water \nvalues will soar to ~150% of normal, which will promote more \nwidespread showers and thunderstorms, with beneficial rainfall \nacross the region. That does not mean every location will see this\nthough. Due to this, the threat of excessive rainfall will also \nincrease, but it is too soon to know exactly where the biggest \nthreat will be. In terms of temperatures, NBM continues to be a \ntad cooler than global ensemble output. However, there is a large \nspread of temperatures between the individual solutions of the \nGEFS and ECMWF, so will continue to monitor.",
